\section*{Preface}
This document serves as an extensive technical guide for the DNA methylation analysis pipeline implemented by Group 4. It integrates theoretical foundations, detailed R code explanations, best practices, and troubleshooting tips to empower rigorous and reproducible Illumina HumanMethylation450K data analysis.

\section{Glossary of Key Terms}

\begin{tcolorbox}[
    breakable,
    colback=white,
    colframe=black!80,
    boxrule=0.7pt,
    arc=4pt,
    left=6pt,
    right=6pt,
    top=6pt,
    bottom=6pt,
    fonttitle=\bfseries\large,
    title=Glossary (Part 1)
]
\raggedright
\begin{description}
    \item[\textbf{p-value}] The probability of obtaining a result as extreme as the observed one under the null hypothesis.
    \item[\textbf{Detection P-value}] Indicates if the signal is distinguishable from background noise (values \textgreater{}0.01 are often unreliable).
    \item[\textbf{Beta-value}] Ratio of methylated to total signal, representing methylation level (range 0–1).
    \item[\textbf{M-value}] Log$_2$ ratio of methylated to unmethylated signal; preferred for statistical analysis.
    \item[\textbf{CpG site}] DNA region where cytosine is followed by guanine; key site for methylation.
    \item[\textbf{Workspace}] The R environment that stores all loaded objects.
    \item[\textbf{Object}] Any data structure stored in R, such as vectors or data frames.
    \item[\textbf{Function}] A block of R code that performs a specific task.
    \item[\textbf{Variable}] A name assigned to an object in R.
    \item[\textbf{Script}] Text file (.R) containing R commands.
    \item[\textbf{Console}] The interactive prompt in RStudio or R.
    \item[\textbf{Data Frame}] A table-like structure with rows and columns.
    \item[\textbf{Vector}] A one-dimensional sequence of data of the same type.
    \item[\textbf{List}] A flexible container that can store different types of objects.
\end{description}
\end{tcolorbox}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\begin{tcolorbox}[
    breakable,
    colback=white,
    colframe=black!80,
    boxrule=0.7pt,
    arc=4pt,
    left=6pt,
    right=6pt,
    top=6pt,
    bottom=6pt,
    fonttitle=\bfseries\large,
    title=Glossary (Part 2)
]
\raggedright
\begin{description}
    \item[\textbf{Loop}] Structure that repeats a block of code.
    \item[\textbf{Operator}] Symbols like \texttt{+}, \texttt{-}, \texttt{\textgreater{}}, \texttt{\textless{}}, \texttt{==} used for operations.
    \item[\textbf{False Discovery Rate (FDR)}] Correction method controlling the expected proportion of false positives.
    \item[\textbf{Dye Bias}] Technical artifact corrected via normalization.
    \item[\textbf{Manifest File}] Metadata file mapping probe IDs to genomic coordinates.
    \item[\textbf{RGChannelSet}] minfi object storing raw red and green intensities.
    \item[\textbf{MethylSet}] minfi object after background correction.
    \item[\textbf{Normalization}] Adjusts data to remove technical biases while preserving biological signal.
    \item[\textbf{Batch Effect}] Unwanted variation introduced by experimental conditions (e.g. date, operator).
    \item[\textbf{Pipeline}] Ordered sequence of analysis steps.
    \item[\textbf{Preprocessing}] Steps like background correction and normalization to prepare data.
    \item[\textbf{Differential Methylation}] Statistical comparison to detect differences in methylation between groups.
    \item[\textbf{Volcano Plot}] Scatter plot showing significance (–log$_{10}$ p-value) vs. effect size.
    \item[\textbf{Manhattan Plot}] Genome-wide plot of –log$_{10}$ p-values by chromosome position.
    \item[\textbf{PCA (Principal Component Analysis)}] Technique to explore variance and detect batch effects.
    \item[\textbf{Pipe Operator (\texttt{\%>\%})}] Operator from magrittr/dplyr to chain commands in readable steps.
\end{description}
\end{tcolorbox}

\section{Theoretical Background}
\subsection*{DNA Methylation}
DNA methylation is an epigenetic modification that occurs when a methyl group is added to the 5' position of cytosine residues within CpG dinucleotides. This modification is crucial for regulating gene expression and maintaining genome stability. Aberrant methylation is associated with diseases such as cancer and neurological disorders.

\subsection*{Illumina 450K BeadChip}
The Illumina HumanMethylation450K BeadChip enables high-throughput analysis of over 485,000 CpG sites across the genome. It uses bisulfite conversion to differentiate between methylated and unmethylated cytosines:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Infinium I}: Two separate probes (one for methylated, one for unmethylated) and single-color detection.
    \item \textbf{Infinium II}: A single probe with two-color detection to distinguish methylation status.
\end{itemize}

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.3}
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{8pt}
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{4cm} p{5.2cm} p{5.2cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Characteristic} & \textbf{Infinium I} & \textbf{Infinium II} \\
\midrule
Number of Probes & Two (one for methylated, one for unmethylated) & One (single probe detects both) \\
Detection Channel & Single color per probe & Dual color within probe \\
CpG Coverage & Slightly lower & Slightly higher \\
Probe Design & Two separate bead types & One bead type \\
Signal Intensity & Higher dynamic range & Lower dynamic range \\
Normalization & Less dye bias, easier normalization & More dye bias, needs adjustment \\
Dye Bias Susceptibility & Lower & Higher \\
Interpretation & Raw signals easier to interpret & Requires correction for dye bias \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Comparison of Infinium I and Infinium II chemistries on the Illumina 450K array.}
\end{table}


\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.7\textwidth]{Screenshot 2025-05-31 085041.png}
    \caption{Example of an Illumina 450K bead chip.}
\end{figure}

\subsection*{Bisulfite Conversion}
Bisulfite treatment converts unmethylated cytosines into uracils (read as thymines after PCR), while methylated cytosines remain unchanged. This differential conversion allows the array probes to detect methylation states.

\section{R — A Deep Dive}
\subsection*{What is R?}
R is a free, open-source programming language and environment specifically designed for statistical computing and data visualization. It is widely used in bioinformatics because of its:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Flexibility in handling large and complex datasets typical of omics studies.
    \item Extensive ecosystem of packages dedicated to genomics and epigenetics (e.g. \texttt{minfi}, \texttt{limma}, \texttt{edgeR}).
    \item Integration with Bioconductor, providing standardized workflows for methylation analysis.
    \item Seamless interface with RStudio, an integrated development environment (IDE) that simplifies script writing, data visualization, and debugging.
\end{itemize}
R is particularly valued for its ability to chain together data preprocessing, statistical testing, visualization, and result export, making it a complete solution for DNA methylation analysis.

\subsection*{The RStudio Interface}
\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=1.0\textwidth]{Screenshot 2025-05-31 113726.png}
    \caption{RStudio Interface — Console, Script Editor, Workspace, Plots.}
\end{figure}

\subsection*{Tips and Useful Commands in R}
To efficiently work with R, it is helpful to know some basic tips and commands:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Use the assignment operator \texttt{<-} to create objects: \texttt{mydata <- read.csv("data.csv")}.
    \item Clean the workspace to avoid object conflicts: \texttt{rm(list = ls())}.
    \item Use the \texttt{\#} symbol to comment and annotate code.
    \item Check installed packages with \texttt{installed.packages()} and load them with \texttt{library(pkgname)}.
    \item Explore data with commands like \texttt{str()}, \texttt{summary()}, \texttt{head()}, and \texttt{tail()}.
    \item Use pipes (\texttt{\%>\%}) from the \texttt{magrittr} or \texttt{dplyr} package to chain commands and improve code readability.
    \item For quick plotting, try \texttt{plot()}, while for more advanced graphics use \texttt{ggplot2}.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Packages and Libraries}
Packages extend R’s functionality. Use:
\begin{tcolorbox}[title=Installing and Loading Packages]
\begin{lstlisting}
install.packages("ggplot2")
library(ggplot2)
if (!requireNamespace("BiocManager", quietly = TRUE))
    install.packages("BiocManager")
BiocManager::install("minfi")
\end{lstlisting}
\end{tcolorbox}

\subsection*{Data Structures in R}
In R, data structures are fundamental for organizing and analyzing data. Here are the most common structures you will encounter in methylation analysis:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Vector}: A one-dimensional sequence of elements of the same type (numeric, character, logical).
    \item \textbf{Factor}: A special type of vector for categorical data with predefined levels.
    \item \textbf{Matrix}: A two-dimensional array of elements of the same type.
    \item \textbf{Data Frame}: A table-like structure with rows and columns, where each column can have a different type.
    \item \textbf{List}: A flexible container that can hold objects of different types and structures (e.g. data frames, vectors, even other lists).
\end{itemize}

Example:
\begin{lstlisting}
# Vector
my_vector <- c(1, 2, 3, 4)

# Factor
my_factor <- factor(c("Control", "Treatment", "Control"))

# Data Frame
my_df <- data.frame(SampleID = c("A1", "A2"), Value = c(0.5, 0.7))

# List
my_list <- list(Numeric = my_vector, Category = my_factor, Table = my_df)
\end{lstlisting}

Understanding these data structures is essential for efficient data manipulation and analysis in R.



\section{About minfi}
The \texttt{minfi} package is a key R/Bioconductor tool for analyzing Illumina methylation arrays. It provides:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Import of raw IDAT files (\texttt{read.metharray.exp()}).
    \item Quality control metrics (e.g. \texttt{detectionP()}).
    \item Preprocessing, including background correction and normalization.
    \item Calculation of Beta-values and M-values.
\end{itemize}
minfi supports downstream statistical analyses and integrates with visualization packages, making it essential for reproducible methylation analysis.

