\section{Pipeline Overview}
An expanded step-by-step pipeline:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Data Import}: Read .idat files with \texttt{read.metharray.exp()}.
    \item \textbf{Annotation}: Use \texttt{getManifest()} to map probe IDs.
    \item \textbf{Quality Control}: Calculate detection p-values (\texttt{detectionP()}).
    \item \textbf{Preprocessing}: Correct background with \texttt{preprocessRaw()} and apply functional normalization with \texttt{preprocessFunnorm()}.
    \item \textbf{Beta/M-value Calculation}: Calculate Beta-values (\texttt{getBeta()}) and M-values (\texttt{getM()}).
    \item \textbf{Statistical Testing}: Use \texttt{t.test()} and \texttt{shapiro.test()}.
    \item \textbf{Multiple Testing Correction}: Apply \texttt{p.adjust()} for FDR.
    \item \textbf{Visualization}: Create plots with \texttt{ggplot2}, \texttt{heatmap.2()}, \texttt{manhattan()}, \texttt{fviz\_pca()}.
\end{enumerate}

\section{The Analytical Pipeline — From Biological Question to Visualization}
\noindent
A successful DNA methylation study requires a well-designed workflow that systematically addresses every analytical step. Below, we outline a typical pipeline, emphasizing its logical flow from hypothesis formulation to data visualization:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Biological Question}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Define the scientific goal: e.g. `Does methylation differ between disease and control groups?''.
        \item Establish hypotheses and experimental design (number of samples, replicates, conditions).
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Sample Collection and Preparation}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Extract DNA from relevant tissues or cell types.
        \item Perform bisulfite conversion to differentiate methylated from unmethylated cytosines.
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Data Acquisition}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Use the Illumina HumanMethylation450K array (or EPIC) to profile methylation.
        \item Generate raw data files (.idat).
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Data Import into R}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Use \texttt{read.metharray.exp()} to read .idat files into RGChannelSet.
        \item Inspect sample metadata to ensure correct group labels.
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Annotation}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Link probe IDs to genomic features using \texttt{getManifest()}.
        \item Exclude problematic probes (e.g. SNP-overlapping, cross-hybridizing).
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Quality Control}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Assess detection p-values (\texttt{detectionP()}) to filter unreliable probes.
        \item Evaluate sample quality (e.g. density plots, boxplots).
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Preprocessing}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Apply background correction (\texttt{preprocessRaw()}).
        \item Normalize data using functional normalization (\texttt{preprocessFunnorm()}) to mitigate technical biases.
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Methylation Quantification}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Calculate Beta-values (\texttt{getBeta()}) and M-values (\texttt{getM()}).
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Statistical Testing}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Test for differential methylation between groups using parametric (\texttt{t.test()}) or non-parametric tests.
        \item Validate assumptions (e.g. normality using \texttt{shapiro.test()}).
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Multiple Testing Correction}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Apply corrections (e.g. FDR via \texttt{p.adjust()}) to control for false discoveries.
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Visualization}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Create boxplots, volcano plots, Manhattan plots, PCA plots to interpret and communicate results.
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Biological Interpretation}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Map significant CpGs to genes and pathways.
        \item Integrate findings with external data (e.g. gene expression, clinical data).
    \end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}

\noindent
This pipeline illustrates how each step builds upon the previous one, transforming raw experimental data into biologically meaningful insights.

