\end{functionbox}

\subsection*{Preprocessing}
\begin{functionbox}
preprocessRaw() — Background correction.\\
preprocessFunnorm() — Functional normalization.
\end{functionbox}

\subsection*{Beta/M-value Calculation}
\begin{functionbox}
getBeta() — Calculates Beta-values.\\
getM() — Calculates M-values.
\end{functionbox}

\section{Tips and Troubleshooting}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Object Not Found}: Check spelling carefully (case-sensitive) and ensure that the object was created in the current R session. Use \texttt{ls()} to list all objects in the workspace.
    \item \textbf{Package Not Loaded}: Always load packages with \texttt{library()} before using their functions. For Bioconductor packages, remember to install via \texttt{BiocManager::install()} first.
    \item \textbf{Dimension Mismatch}: Check that data frames or matrices used in functions have the same number of rows/columns or match the expected structure. Use \texttt{dim()} and \texttt{head()} to inspect.
    \item \textbf{Warning Messages}: R issues warnings (e.g. \texttt{Warning: NAs introduced by coercion}) that do not stop code but may indicate potential problems. Review warnings carefully with \texttt{warnings()} and adjust code accordingly.
    \item \textbf{Missing Values (NAs)}: Check for missing values with \texttt{is.na()} and decide on appropriate handling (e.g. remove or impute).
    \item \textbf{Normalization Issues}: If downstream analyses seem off (e.g. unexpected sample clustering or dye bias), verify that background correction and normalization have been correctly applied. Re-check steps like \texttt{preprocessRaw()} and \texttt{preprocessFunnorm()}.
    \item \textbf{Dye Bias}: A technical artifact especially in Infinium II probes. Use appropriate normalization methods to correct (e.g. functional normalization).
    \item \textbf{Version Conflicts}: Make sure R and packages are updated to avoid deprecated functions. Check package version with \texttt{packageVersion("packagename")}.
    \item \textbf{Visualization Errors}: If plots look wrong or incomplete, check that the data is correctly structured and complete. Use \texttt{ggplot2} syntax carefully.
    \item \textbf{Unexpected Results}: Always verify that the correct samples and group labels were assigned during preprocessing. Use \texttt{table()} to cross-check factors like group and batch.
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.3cm}


\section{Conclusion}
This manual is designed to guide users through the process of DNA methylation data analysis, from data import to visualization and interpretation. Our project demonstrates the critical role of bioinformatics in modern molecular biology and highlights the power of reproducible research. By using robust tools like R and minfi, we can process large datasets efficiently and gain valuable insights into epigenetic regulation and disease mechanisms.

The expected results include the identification of differentially methylated regions (DMRs) that may serve as biomarkers for disease, as well as the potential discovery of new epigenetic patterns associated with clinical outcomes. This work contributes to the broader goal of personalized medicine, where DNA methylation signatures can inform diagnostics and therapeutic strategies.

We hope this document empowers other researchers to replicate our pipeline and adapt it to their own datasets, fostering collaboration and accelerating discoveries in the field of epigenetics.


